that we look at this was usually related to data center equipment so compute

05:20.380 --> 05:24.940
platforms like servers but also appliances security appliances and even

05:24.940 --> 05:31.460
HSMs so this is a wide range and I'm actually convinced that the bootloaders

05:31.460 --> 05:37.380
that we will talk about here will actually extend and cover a wider range

05:37.380 --> 05:43.800
of devices like IOT and cars and most likely you will be recognizing some of

05:43.800 --> 05:50.840
open source bootloaders as well. Now I will kick this off with one of the first vulnerabilities

05:50.840 --> 06:00.680
that I found in Cisco devices. So this is a Cisco Nexus N9000 series switch. There's different

06:00.680 --> 06:08.120
variations, different port counts, but inside they all look similar if you open them up, because

06:08.840 --> 06:13.320
there's a lot of specialized hardware like ASICs that handle network traffic of course.

06:13.800 --> 06:17.120
But it's all bootstrapped by this, what I call the management board,

06:17.780 --> 06:19.760
shown here on the right-hand picture.

06:20.900 --> 06:25.160
So in this picture, you should be able to see an SSD down there.

06:25.620 --> 06:28.300
And under the heatsink, there's an Intel processor.

06:29.400 --> 06:33.580
And Cisco actually invested, you know, some resources there

06:33.580 --> 06:34.980
and hardened the boot chain.

06:35.440 --> 06:37.180
So this device has a secure boot chain.

06:37.180 --> 06:38.740
It has a root-of-trust chip.

06:39.860 --> 06:43.620
And, yeah, you could consider it state-of-the-art, in my opinion.

06:43.800 --> 06:48.320
And, well, of course, I as an attacker don't like this.

06:48.320 --> 06:50.720
I want to run my own software on this.

06:50.720 --> 06:53.680
I want to run Doom or whatever.

06:53.680 --> 06:58.000
And since it's an Intel processor, I would expect to find Intel firmware.

06:58.000 --> 06:59.440
And actually, that's what I did.

06:59.440 --> 07:03.160
So I took out the mainboard, turned it around, and there it is.

07:03.160 --> 07:06.240
There you have your small SPI flash chip.

07:06.240 --> 07:09.560
This is very common in these types of devices.

07:09.560 --> 07:15.120
And when you dump it and open it in UEFI tool, you find that this is a firmware volume.

07:15.440 --> 07:20.240
There's a couple of firmware volumes on there with all sorts of drivers and EDK2-based firmware.

07:20.580 --> 07:22.860
And one of them is actually called Cisco Grub.

07:23.200 --> 07:30.480
And since I already knew they had implemented secure boot, I thought maybe Cisco Grub actually is the bootloader.

07:30.480 --> 07:36.380
And maybe it's involved in enforcing secure boot and signature checks for the operating system image.

07:36.380 --> 07:46.580
So I fired up Ghidra, loaded up this binary, and what I found were some strings that seemed a little odd, right?

07:46.760 --> 07:50.860
And it actually seemed like they had just taken grub.

07:51.280 --> 07:57.440
But I expected to see grub too, which is like the modern version of it and the one that all Linux distribution use nowadays.

07:58.000 --> 08:05.280
But instead, after googling some of the strings, I found that they had actually forked grub legacy, the one from the 90s.

08:05.280 --> 08:08.320
and that was surprising.

08:09.160 --> 08:10.920
So then I thought, you know,

08:12.020 --> 08:14.700
how on earth did they actually implement Secure Boot

08:14.700 --> 08:16.020
with such an old bootloader?

08:16.300 --> 08:17.960
Grub legacy didn't support Secure Boot.

08:17.960 --> 08:21.640
So the way that this works is Cisco Grub

08:21.640 --> 08:23.860
will identify all kinds of boot devices.

08:24.420 --> 08:25.900
This is mostly the internal flash

08:25.900 --> 08:28.560
that you saw on the previous image,

08:28.820 --> 08:30.500
but it could also be a USB drive

08:30.500 --> 08:32.700
that you plug into the front of the device.

08:32.700 --> 08:38.700
And once it's identified a bootable device, it will try and identify OS images.

08:38.700 --> 08:41.700
They always kind of look the same and have some signatures on them.

08:41.700 --> 08:49.700
And once it's loaded in an operating system image, it will verify the signature and execute it, if it's proper.

08:49.700 --> 08:55.700
This first part here, this actually requires interacting with a file system.

08:55.700 --> 09:11.860
And the way that this is commonly solved today in modern firmware is that you use EDK2 or load image which hooks into the EFI firmware where someone else has implemented a way to interact with some file system and load a file.

09:12.540 --> 09:17.100
But Grub Legacy didn't have all that because it's from the 90s, right?

09:17.100 --> 09:19.380
So they implemented all kinds of file system drivers.

09:19.380 --> 09:24.000
and what actually happens here is that there's a big loop

09:24.000 --> 09:27.400
that basically brute forces all available file system backends

09:27.400 --> 09:29.940
and the first one that mounts successfully

09:29.940 --> 09:33.780
will actually be used to try and find an operating system image.

09:36.100 --> 09:40.500
My idea was maybe I can find a bug in such a file system

09:40.500 --> 09:43.920
because at the time they had around 12 or 13

09:43.920 --> 09:47.380
like random file systems like even RyzaFS

09:47.380 --> 09:51.360
So I thought, okay, there might be actually a chance for me to find a vulnerability.

09:52.000 --> 09:58.720
And looking at the XFS driver, I found this function that's responsible for listing files.

09:59.460 --> 10:05.620
And here you can see there's this link buff, stack allocated buffer, that has a size.

10:05.740 --> 10:08.800
And its size is controlled by a variable in the super block.

10:09.180 --> 10:12.340
And you need to remember that the super block is actually stored externally.

10:12.760 --> 10:15.980
It's stored on a USB drive or the internal SSD.

10:15.980 --> 10:20.980
And of course, if I have physical access, or maybe even remote access with a root shell,

10:20.980 --> 10:26.020
I can just modify that superblock, set its size to zero, which will make the next length

10:26.020 --> 10:34.600
check here succeed, and end up calling memcopy into my zero size link buff buffer with an

10:34.600 --> 10:36.120
attacker-controlled size.

10:36.120 --> 10:40.120
So this is a textbook example of a buffer overflow.

10:40.120 --> 10:44.000
Of course, there's no exploit mitigations or anything like that.

10:44.000 --> 10:48.560
And I think there's two scenarios in which you can exploit this.

10:48.560 --> 10:54.820
The first one is what I've hinted at already, like you can plug a USB drive that you prepared

10:54.820 --> 11:00.520
into the front of the device that's shown here on this picture and, you know, just walk

11:00.520 --> 11:06.400
up to it, plug it in, and the next time the device will reboot, it could trigger the vulnerability.

11:06.400 --> 11:11.200
Or you could do that remotely, you know, maybe you have administrative credentials or another

11:11.200 --> 11:14.080
a vulnerability, you can get a shell.

11:14.080 --> 11:15.360
This is actually a feature.

11:15.360 --> 11:18.160
You can just ask the device to give you a shell

11:18.160 --> 11:20.900
and then use that shell to write the malicious XFF partition

11:20.900 --> 11:22.480
to disk and reboot.

11:22.480 --> 11:25.760
And what happens then is that Grub will, or Cisco Grub,

11:25.760 --> 11:27.740
will open a well-known file.

11:27.740 --> 11:30.100
And you can use that to exploit the buffer overflow, get code

11:30.100 --> 11:34.260
execution, bypass the signature checks, and so on.

11:34.260 --> 11:37.300
If you actually do this, it would look similar to this.

11:37.300 --> 11:39.100
I did this for demonstration purposes here.

11:39.100 --> 11:40.960
This is actually in an emulator, where

11:40.960 --> 11:43.100
you get a nice register dump at the end.

11:43.100 --> 11:45.860
But basically, I set the storage device.

11:45.860 --> 11:49.400
I list a file, and this triggers the buffer overflow

11:49.400 --> 11:52.940
in the XFS file system backend.

11:52.940 --> 11:53.440
Cool.

11:56.500 --> 11:58.500
What does this allow you to do?

11:58.500 --> 12:02.560
You get code execution in the context of Cisco Grub.

12:02.560 --> 12:03.500
That's very privileged.

12:03.500 --> 12:05.300
There's no exploit mitigations.

12:05.300 --> 12:07.480
I find this very hard to detect, honestly,

12:07.480 --> 12:10.880
because the device doesn't really give you that many ways

12:10.880 --> 12:12.800
that you could detect an attack like that.

12:12.800 --> 12:14.800
Yes, they have a root of trust chip,

12:14.800 --> 12:19.760
but even if it did gather measurements or hashes

12:19.760 --> 12:22.600
of the firmware and stores them in a safe place,

12:22.600 --> 12:24.280
there's no software interface that you can use

12:24.280 --> 12:27.520
to interface with that root of trust chip yet.

12:27.520 --> 12:33.440
And also, all recovery functions that this device provides

12:33.440 --> 12:38.440
are based or implemented in either Grub itself,

12:38.440 --> 12:40.940
maybe some other drivers, but that's unlikely,

12:40.940 --> 12:44.580
or most of it actually lives in the Linux operating system

12:44.580 --> 12:47.000
that has started after you already compromised Grub.

12:47.000 --> 12:49.900
So if you're smart about it, you could potentially

12:49.900 --> 12:52.100
try and hook into these recovery functions as well

12:52.100 --> 12:55.700
and gain a stealthy foothold to whichever network

12:55.700 --> 12:57.700
that device is deployed in.

12:57.700 --> 12:59.720
Or often these devices are also used

12:59.720 --> 13:02.720
to run certain applications that have credentials.

13:02.720 --> 13:09.320
And, yeah, this is cool as long as you, again, have credentials to the device or physical access.

13:10.040 --> 13:11.520
And it really doesn't take that long.

13:11.660 --> 13:14.380
Like, plugging in a USB drive and letting it boot takes, like, three minutes.

13:15.760 --> 13:19.900
I would like to thank Cisco for addressing these vulnerabilities and fixing them.

