version iDRAC 9 is what I reviewed, and it's used across all sorts of products. So I'm

15:21.700 --> 15:25.280
going to refer to servers, I'm going to show you pictures of servers, but actually this

15:25.280 --> 15:30.440
is also used in storage appliances and all sorts of other things, and they reused a lot

15:30.440 --> 15:37.820
of it, actually the same SOC, and the amount of reuse is really nice for me as an attacker

15:37.820 --> 15:44.320
of course, but of course underlines the criticality of this device actually executing only code

15:44.320 --> 15:46.680
that was signed by Dell.

15:46.680 --> 15:53.460
So they implemented secure boot where, again, each stage verifies the next one, and yeah,

15:53.460 --> 16:00.400
then hopefully you end up running Linux on this separate system that, you know, has signatures

16:00.400 --> 16:05.060
and even integrity enforced by the fault system.

16:05.060 --> 16:13.520
Now, it turns out that they are using an SOC dedicated to BMC use cases created by Nuvotan.

16:14.300 --> 16:18.220
And Nuvotan also wrote what's called a first-stage bootloader called BootBlock.

16:18.460 --> 16:19.820
That's just their project name.

16:21.160 --> 16:22.760
And I found a vulnerability in BootBlock.

16:23.760 --> 16:28.740
So there's one function, basically, that has a lot of the business logic.

16:28.740 --> 16:36.620
A first-stage bootloader basically has one job, and that is to bring the system into

16:36.620 --> 16:40.260
a state where a more capable bootloader can execute.

16:40.260 --> 16:46.940
So this function here tries to locate the image of that next more capable bootloader

16:46.940 --> 16:54.120
in external memory, in an SPI flash, load it into DRAM, verify signature, and then execute

16:54.120 --> 16:55.120
it.

16:55.120 --> 16:57.560
That's what this function is supposed to do.

16:57.560 --> 17:04.060
So as you can see here, I've already highlighted that there's different locations in memory

17:04.060 --> 17:05.800
where you could put this bootloader.

17:05.800 --> 17:10.320
Maybe the next bootloader prefers to be executed from a specific address, well in that case

17:10.320 --> 17:15.880
you just modify the image header of that bootloader such that it is copied by bootblock into that

17:15.880 --> 17:17.600
particular address.

17:17.600 --> 17:23.220
And well, I as an attacker end up controlling that address because it's stored in the external

17:23.220 --> 17:26.000
SPI flash, and the signature of that whole thing

17:26.000 --> 17:30.500
is only verified after it has been loaded to that destination

17:30.500 --> 17:31.220
address.

17:31.220 --> 17:37.220
So my idea here was that I could try and fool boot

17:37.220 --> 17:40.840
block into overriding itself with my payload.

17:40.840 --> 17:44.780
So you need to know that SRAM is just a different type

17:44.780 --> 17:48.780
of memory that's addressable like any other memory in the SOC.

17:48.780 --> 17:50.520
That's where boot block lives.

17:50.520 --> 17:53.780
Boot block will execute the check image copy and jump function.

17:53.780 --> 17:59.000
It will read the header from external memory that I control.

17:59.000 --> 18:02.700
I can guess the address of boot block in memory

18:02.700 --> 18:05.400
and have mem copy overwrite the rest of boot block.

18:05.400 --> 18:06.240
Why does this work?

18:06.240 --> 18:08.860
Because here, actually, conveniently, mem copy

18:08.860 --> 18:10.380
lives at a very high address.

18:10.380 --> 18:13.260
So I can basically overwrite the rest of boot block

18:13.260 --> 18:14.940
without actually overwriting mem copy,

18:14.940 --> 18:16.760
because sometimes this creates issues.

18:16.760 --> 18:19.060
In this case, it was not an issue.

18:19.060 --> 18:23.240
There were a couple of other issues, but if you experiment a bit,

18:23.240 --> 18:24.460
you can get this attack right.

18:24.460 --> 18:29.260
And when memcopy returns, you can return to your own payload.

18:29.260 --> 18:34.540
Because this is exactly the image that you store in external memory.

18:34.540 --> 18:37.760
Yeah, and as you can see, DRAM here is not used at all.

18:37.760 --> 18:43.060
In practice, as an attacker, you would go to a server, open it up,

18:43.060 --> 18:47.060
and find iDrag's SPI flash,

18:47.060 --> 18:50.000
which is indicated by this blue arrow here.

18:50.000 --> 18:53.900
And the right-hand picture shows my experimental setup

