25:57.840 --> 26:04.480
the bugs is is very classic i think so these are puzzle bugs that you know cause memory corruption

26:04.480 --> 26:10.720
issues which brings us to the next point that even if we have perfect threat modeling we probably

26:10.720 --> 26:15.920
want our hardware to support this a little bit we want to use modern hardware security features

26:15.920 --> 26:21.920
that we can use to our advantage as defenders which means that you know secure boot is nice

26:21.920 --> 26:31.180
But over time, there will be bugs in any firmware, in any bootloader perhaps, and it's important

26:31.180 --> 26:37.740
that we actually have a way to manage the versions that are running in our fleets.

26:37.740 --> 26:40.320
And downgrade protection is one way that you can do this.

26:40.320 --> 26:47.300
Imagine if here version 1.1 was affected by critical vulnerability, downgrade protection

26:47.300 --> 26:50.920
could help us prevent an attacker with physical access

26:50.920 --> 26:54.860
