27:07.120 --> 27:09.600
to completely roll out the possibility of this ever

27:09.600 --> 27:11.200
being exploited in your fleet.

27:11.200 --> 27:16.460
And these are devices that are deployed for years, right?

27:16.460 --> 27:18.460
It's one way to solve it.

27:18.460 --> 27:23.640
There is possibly other cases that actually

27:23.640 --> 27:25.900
lead you to want to downgrade.

27:25.900 --> 27:29.640
So it would be nice if this was configurable as a user.

27:29.640 --> 27:31.700
Currently, this is not the case with any device

27:31.700 --> 27:32.380
that we have reviewed.

27:35.100 --> 27:36.800
Another option that would allow you

27:36.800 --> 27:41.100
to manage your firmware vulnerability footprint

27:41.100 --> 27:44.240
or version footprint would be remote attestation.

27:44.240 --> 27:48.060
So secure boot works in the way that one stage verifies the next one.

27:48.440 --> 27:48.940
It's simple.

27:49.200 --> 27:53.120
If the next stage signature doesn't match, if there's something wrong going on,

27:53.500 --> 27:55.120
it would just not execute that.

27:55.120 --> 27:56.980
And this is an important property.

27:57.120 --> 27:57.920
This is strong.

27:58.780 --> 28:01.820
But at the end, if the device actually boots,

28:02.500 --> 28:05.240
you kind of need to infer that it's in a trustworthy state

28:05.240 --> 28:07.240
just from the fact that it booted.

28:07.760 --> 28:10.820
And I think that's a little weak.

28:10.920 --> 28:12.200
I think that it could be better.

28:12.200 --> 28:16.320
and remote attestation is one way that you can use to address this.

28:16.500 --> 28:21.960
You can have each stage not just verify the signature of the next stage.

28:22.140 --> 28:23.420
You can still do that if you like.

28:23.840 --> 28:27.040
But instead, in what's called measured boot,

28:27.240 --> 28:34.020
each stage would collect some evidence of whatever configuration or binary it executes next.

28:34.980 --> 28:40.420
And this evidence, it's usually a hash that you put in a temper-resistant environment

28:40.420 --> 28:44.420
like a root of trust chip or a security chip, some area in your SOC, whatever,

28:45.140 --> 28:50.340
and combined with an identity, a cryptographic identity, you can create proof.

28:50.340 --> 28:55.620
And this proof can then be made, you know, maybe challenged with some freshness

28:55.620 --> 29:01.540
and then used by your credential infrastructure to not just get some evidence of what versions

29:01.540 --> 29:04.420
and what types of firmware and binaries you're running in your fleet,

29:04.420 --> 29:08.900
but also, you know, see if a downgrade actually happens.

29:08.900 --> 29:14.680
because often you just see the changes, and it's not easy to predict what type of evidence you have.

29:15.200 --> 29:17.400
At the end of the day, you just have a bunch of hashes.

29:17.880 --> 29:21.080
So there's a lot of practical hurdles with this,

29:21.720 --> 29:27.760
and maybe one way that we can improve this is by asking vendors to actually make this usable.

29:28.300 --> 29:31.540
In almost all devices that we reviewed, it's actually not usable,

29:31.680 --> 29:34.160
even though the hardware theoretically supports it.

29:34.160 --> 29:39.600
And then if it is usable, it would be nice if it was usable for everyone

29:39.600 --> 29:46.040
and not just large enterprises who already have some kind of credential infrastructure,

29:46.040 --> 29:49.640
who already know what remote attestation is and how it works and so on.

29:51.160 --> 29:53.800
But it is certainly a way to address some of the issues

29:53.800 --> 29:57.120
